---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyFigure from '../components/CaseStudyFigure.astro';

const nav = [
  { label: 'Problem', href: '#problem' },
  { label: 'Solution', href: '#solution' },
  { label: 'Design decisions', href: '#decisions' },
  { label: 'Next steps', href: '#next-steps' },
];

// Placeholders mark where the Figma deck's visuals go; the slide number is in each comment.
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Sequence"
  heading="Making product motion reel as easy as storyboarding"
  role="Product Designer"
  team="Rayna Lai"
  timeline="5 days, October 2026"
  projectType="Product Design, Concept"
  nav={nav}
  commentId="case-study-sequence"
>
  <CaseStudyFigure
    slot="hero"
    src="videos/sequence/title-card.mp4"
    poster="images/case-study-heroes/sequence.jpg"
    alt="Sequence title film: a designer's storyboard of shots turning into a launch video"
    loop
    wide
  />

  <section id="problem" data-comment-id="sequence-problem">
    <h2 class="section-label">Problem</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">
      Product designers without motion experience struggle to turn their designs into launch videos.
      Pro tools take time to learn, simpler ones don’t think in stories, and AI drafts are hard to
      refine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="solution" data-comment-id="sequence-solution">
    <h2 class="section-label">Solution</h2>
    <h3 class="heading text-ink">Designers storyboard shots on a canvas and group them into chapters.</h3>
    {/* Slides 11 and 15: storyboard canvas and chapters recordings */}
    <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: shots laid out as storyboards, grouped into chapters (slides 11, 15)" placeholder wide />

    <h3 class="heading text-ink">They shape the motion by prompting, annotating, or editing properties.</h3>
    {/* Slides 18–20: ask the agent, annotate, edit properties recordings */}
    <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: asking the agent, annotating a shot, then editing properties (slides 18–20)" placeholder wide />

    <p>
      Rayna and I built it in five days, prototyping the key interactions rather than a full editor:
      storyboard editing works, and the AI agent is simulated.
      <a href="https://svsd-nine.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link">Try the prototype</a>
      (desktop only).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="decisions" data-comment-id="sequence-decisions">
    <h2 class="section-label">Design decisions</h2>
    <ol>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Storyboards, not a timeline</h3>
          <p>
            Designers already think in frames and artboards. Laying shots out on a canvas keeps the
            story visible, where timeline tools make you think in elements appearing over time.
          </p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 5: Jitter / Figma Motion timeline */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: a timeline-based motion tool (slide 5)" placeholder />
      </li>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Two levels of control</h3>
          <p>
            Like Figma’s prototyping connections, editable connectors set how one shot transitions
            into the next. Within a shot, designers select elements and adjust their properties.
          </p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 13: connectors and properties */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: editing a connector between shots (slide 13)" placeholder />
      </li>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Chapters for the bigger picture</h3>
          <p>
            Inspired by motion breakdown videos, chapters group shots into sections like an intro,
            walkthrough, or call to action, so designers keep the whole flow in mind.
          </p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 14: motion breakdown inspiration */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: motion breakdown video inspiration (slide 14)" placeholder />
      </li>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Progressive control, not fewer controls</h3>
          <p>
            Too few controls feel limiting; too many feel overwhelming. Designers start with a prompt
            and get more precise only when they need to, without prompting every change or learning a
            pro animation tool.
          </p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 17: too few vs too many controls */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: a sparse tool next to a dense pro animation tool (slide 17)" placeholder />
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="next-steps" data-comment-id="sequence-next-steps">
    <h2 class="section-label">Next steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Bring existing work in</h3>
          <p>Import designs and sketches, and keep them editable once they’re in the workspace.</p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 26: inputs panel */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: importing files, websites, and apps into Sequence (slide 26)" placeholder />
      </li>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Easier ways to iterate</h3>
          <p>Branch to compare creative directions side by side without losing work.</p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 27: branching */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: comparing creative directions side by side (slide 27)" placeholder />
      </li>
      <li class="decision-row">
        <div class="decision-text">
          <h3>Easier ways to edit</h3>
          <p>Refine motion in plain language, without animation jargon or metrics.</p>
        </div>
        {/* Slide 28: animation log */}
        <CaseStudyFigure alt="Placeholder: refining motion in plain language (slide 28)" placeholder />
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .decision-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .decision-row {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      align-items: start;
    }
  }

  .decision-row :global(.case-study-figure) {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .decision-row :global(.case-study-figure) {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
